name: Destructive empty-roles UPDATE reachable from UI in three clicks (Edit Owner → remove all role TagItems → Save) with NO confirmation
canonical_in_docs: false
canonical_candidate: true
pillar_affinity:
  - P-08
  - P-09
description: |-
  **NEW HIGH-severity BUG-CLASS INVARIANT, batch 2026-05-20-Q (OwnersList
  primary source)** — UI-tier elevation of the existing batch-P
  `update-owner-empty-roles-silently-deletes-all-bindings` invariant
  (which was scoped to "API-consumer-only hazard; UI in practice
  always sends the current roles list"). This sidecar's primary-
  source evidence ESCALATES the hazard: the destructive default is
  REACHABLE FROM THE OPERATOR UI in three clicks.

  **The three clicks**:

    1. Click `Edit` on an Owner row in `/management/owners`
       (`EditableOwnerItem.tsx:41-54` — gated by `OWNER_UPDATE`
       permission, hidden when absent). The OwnerForm dialog opens
       with the Owner's existing roles rendered as TagItems
       (`OwnerForm.tsx:95-103`).

    2. Click the X icon on each TagItem to remove all role bindings
       (`OwnerForm.tsx:101` — `onRemoveClick={handleRemove(idx)}`).
       The `useFieldArray` (`OwnerForm.tsx:40-43`) has NO validation
       rules on the `roles` field — `formState.isValid` is gated by
       the `name` field's `rules={{ required: true, validate: value
       => !!value.trim() }}` (`OwnerForm.tsx:77`) and remains TRUE
       throughout the removal sequence.

    3. Click `Save` (`OwnerForm.tsx:108-117`) — the Save button is
       enabled because `formState.isValid === true`. The submission
       dispatches `updateOwner({ ownerId, ownerFormData })` with
       `roles: []`.

  **The destructive consequence** (per the batch-P invariant
  `update-owner-empty-roles-silently-deletes-all-bindings`):
  `OwnerServiceImpl.update` (lines 71, 76-81, 117-122) calls
  `deleteOwnerRelationsExcept(ownerId, List.of()).then(createRelations(ownerId, List.of()))`.
  The Postgres empty-NOT-IN treats all existing role-links as
  matching, so ALL role bindings are HARD-DELETED from
  `owner_to_role`. The change is invisible to the platform's audit
  trail (per the `no-audit-log-on-rbac-mutations` 6-sidecar pattern
  including this UI tier).

  **The asymmetry between UX choices**:
    - The DELETE-Owner verb has a `ConfirmationDialog` with the
      warning "<owner-name> will be deleted permanently"
      (`EditableOwnerItem.tsx:55-72`). The DELETE is SOFT (per F-019
      batch-P — `deleted_at` timestamp, name re-usable via partial
      unique index).
    - The UPDATE-with-empty-roles role-strip has NO confirmation
      modal, NO red-warning banner, NO before/after diff. The
      role-bindings are HARD-DELETED (irreversible — `owner_to_role`
      rows are physically removed, no soft-delete column on the
      edge table per F-019 `hard-delete-on-relationship-edges-confirmed-cross-batch`).
    - The MORE-REVERSIBLE operation (delete) carries a confirmation
      barrier; the MORE-DESTRUCTIVE operation (role-strip) does not.

  **Why HIGH severity**: this is the operator-facing trigger of an
  irreversible role-stripping operation with no audit trail. The
  combination of:
    (a) UI reachability in three clicks;
    (b) no confirmation modal;
    (c) no audit log (`no-audit-log-on-rbac-mutations` 6-sidecar
        pattern);
    (d) hard-delete on the `owner_to_role` edges (irreversible);
    (e) the soft-delete-on-Owner-row semantic (the Owner survives
        but is role-less);
  means that an operator (or anyone shoulder-surfing an operator's
  session) can silently strip all role bindings from any Owner with
  zero confirmation, zero forensic trace anywhere except the
  `owner_to_role` row-count delta.

  **The mitigation surface is the OwnerForm dialog**: adding a
  confirmation modal "Are you sure you want to remove ALL role
  bindings from <Owner-name>? This cannot be undone." gated on the
  `roles.length === 0` condition is a 5-line UI change. The deeper
  fix is at the API layer — interpret omitted `roles` as "preserve"
  per the batch-P invariant's mitigation list.

  **Cross-batch coherence (LSN-018)**: this sidecar STRENGTHENS the
  batch-P `update-owner-empty-roles-silently-deletes-all-bindings`
  invariant by establishing the operator-facing trigger surface. It
  ALSO strengthens the `no-audit-log-on-rbac-mutations` 6-sidecar
  pattern by adding the UI tier as the seventh corroborating evidence
  point — the UI affords the operation, no audit captures it, the
  destruction is irreversible.
