mitigations_unimplemented:
  - |-
    UI-side (lowest cost): add a `ConfirmationDialog` wrapper in
    `OwnerForm` that intercepts Save when `roles.length === 0 &&
    previousRoles.length > 0`. Warning text: "This will remove ALL
    role bindings from <Owner-name> permanently. This cannot be
    undone. Continue?" — gated by a "Yes, remove all roles" button.
  - |-
    UI-side (UX nicety): show a red "warning" banner on the form
    when the operator removes the last TagItem — "Removing the last
    role will strip all permissions from this Owner."
  - |-
    API-side (definitive fix): the batch-P invariant's mitigation
    list — mark `roles` as REQUIRED in the OpenAPI contract, OR
    introduce a PATCH endpoint for partial updates, OR interpret
    omitted/null roles as "preserve".
  - |-
    Doc-side: extend `/configuration-and-deployment/enable-security/authorization/owners`
    with explicit guidance: "Editing an Owner with no role bindings
    permanently removes all role attachments. Always retain at least
    one role binding unless you intend the Owner to have zero
    permissions."
  - |-
    Audit-side: pair with the `no-audit-log-on-rbac-mutations`
    mitigation — wire `@ActivityLog(OWNER_UPDATED)` on
    OwnerServiceImpl.update so role-rebinding leaves a trace.
notes: |-
  The asymmetry (destructive-update has no confirmation, less-destructive
  delete has one) is the FIRST UX-level evidence that the platform's
  UX convention "delete needs confirmation; edit does not" was applied
  without reasoning about which operation is actually more destructive
  in the platform's semantics.

